@meta
id: 08-07
title: Delete is a cut, not a shred
chapter: 08
type: lesson
skills: delete-d, put-p
par: 13
minutes: 4
vscode: Alt-Up, or cut the line and paste it somewhere else
open: order.entity.ts

@brief
Here is the thing nobody tells you on day one: vim has no separate cut
command, because delete already is one. Everything `x`, `dd` and their
relatives remove goes into a register and stays there until the next
delete or yank overwrites it.

So moving a line is not "delete, then retype". It is "delete, then put it
back somewhere else", and the second half is a single key.

`customerId` belongs directly under `id` in this entity — the codebase
orders identifiers first, then state. Move it. Do not retype it.

@keys
| `dd` | delete the line into the register |
| `p` | put the register back *after* the cursor line |
| `P` | put it *before* the cursor line |
| `k` | up a line |

@hints
The line you want is not lost after you delete it. Get it out of the way
first and then worry about where it goes.
--
`p` puts the last thing you deleted or yanked back into the file. For a
line, that means a whole new line below the one you are on. `P` puts it
above.
--
`3j` `dd` takes `customerId`. You are now on `total`. Go up to `status`
and `P` puts the line above it.

@solution
    3j dd  k P  :wq<Enter>

Six keystrokes to move a line, and the line itself is never retyped, so
it cannot pick up a typo on the way.

Two things follow. The first is practical: if you delete something by
accident you have `u`, and if you deleted it on purpose and then changed
your mind about where it goes, `p` is still holding it.

The second is a warning. There is only one unnamed register, and every
delete writes to it — including `x`. Delete a line, then delete a stray
character before you put, and the character is what you get back. When a
move goes wrong that is almost always why. Chapter 20 gives you twenty-six
named registers to keep things apart; until then, put first, tidy after.

@start order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  id: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
  customerId: string;
  total: number;
};

@expected order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
  total: number;
};
